VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'thought it might look better if Captain Picard is in his actual Starfleet uniform. So, let me minimize this one here, and I\'ll play the other version of it that I think rolls a little better here. So, let\'s hit the play button and see how this one came out.'

[04:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Not him again. The speed limit here is 1/4 impulse, not warp 1. &gt;&gt; But, I did find it struggles with dialogue when you have longer clips. So, for example, I had a 10-second clip'

[04:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'generated with a different revision. Take a look. &gt;&gt; Not him again. &gt;&gt; We\'re not doing this again, Darth. &gt;&gt; Resistance is futile, Captain. Make it'

[04:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so. &gt;&gt; So, I have found that it struggles having two characters on screen communicating with each other. They can interact with each other visually, but the characters tend to get their voices mixed up. But, perhaps I\'m not prompting'

[05:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it correctly. I\'m just getting into this today. But, it does look like it can handle motion graphics. So, for example, I asked it to make a looping background here. Of course, I didn\'t give it much of a prompt, so it\'s got some gibberish text here. But, as you can see here, it'

[05:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is looping properly. I did ask it to make sure the first and last frame line up so it can do things properly. Now, of course, it cheated by just having a blank blue square there at the beginning and end, but still, this does kind of'

[05:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'work as a looping background that if I was more specific with the prompt, I\'m sure I could get it to work a little better. So, there\'s a lot of different things that this model is able to do, again, running on hardware that sits on your desk. Now, it can also work with'

[05:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'still images. You can take a single image and turn it into video, which is what I did with this example. So, this is the output that it put together. There is some music that runs underneath it, and you can see the little guy there'

[05:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'coming to life, and she\'s going to throw him onto the floor, and he\'s going to run away in the second shot. Pretty cool stuff. And you can also use in this model an ending image. So, what you can do is take a before and an after and'

[06:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have the model merge the two together, kind of bridge them with a video. Now, there\'s a lot more to this ComfyUI. As you can see, it\'s got a node-based interface here, so you can do a lot more than just have a prompt. You can wire up'

[06:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all these different things together and make much more complex outputs with different orders of operation and everything. So, there is a lot more that I could probably do to improve everything you just saw here. So, here\'s'

[06:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'another one that I did earlier. I took a picture of a Nintendo 64 cartridge to see what it could do with that. &gt;&gt; Now, you\'re playing with power. Lawn power. &gt;&gt; So, I\'m really impressed with the quality of what I\'m seeing out of this'

[06:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'model. This is freely available up on Hugging Face. I\'ll talk more about how I installed all this stuff in a few minutes. And one of the real benefits, I think, of using a local model for video generation is that if you wanted to use'

[07:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a more powerful model, you can experiment with your prompts locally and iterate them and get them to a point where they\'re starting to generate the things that you\'re looking for, and then take that prompt that you iterated'

[07:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'through your local hardware, and then send it up to the much more expensive cloud-based model to get the high-quality output that you might be after. And I think that\'s one of the real benefits here. One of the frustrations I had when Google\'s VEO'

[07:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'came out was that I could only run four or five iterations before I would use up all of my tokens for the month. Here, I can experiment over and over again. It doesn\'t take all that long to generate clips. Again, I can\'t go too long with'

[07:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'clip generation, but generally, depending on the length that I\'m trying to get out of it, in about two to five minutes, I\'ve got something that I can look at and then iterate upon. So, the wait times are not all that extensive'

[07:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'here. And if I had two GPUs, I think it would be a little bit faster. And as I was yapping away here, I had the model make an anime version of what we just put together here. Is this amazing? So, it\'s pretty cool and a lot of fun to'

[08:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'play with and I think it\'s got a lot of potential that I have yet to figure out. So, that will be next on the docket. to give you an idea as to how fast are moving here, just today as I was trying to figure out all of this stuff, a new'

